course: everyday
lesson: 384
title: When the police come
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The knock came at teatime. A police officer stood on Kieran's doorstep asking about a bike stolen from outside the Co-op. Kieran's mum went white, then red. Kieran had not taken it, but he had been there, laughing, while an older boy rode off. The officer asked calm, plain questions and wrote Kieran's name in her notebook. «Am I arrested?» Kieran squeaked. «No,» she said, «but now I know who was there. Let's keep it that way — just a name, not a record.» His mum didn't speak to him all evening, which was worse than shouting.
explain: That same month Jake knocked over a fruit stand outside a shop during a scrap. Oranges rolled into the road, and the shopkeeper, Mr Patel, fed up, rang the police. Jake had to meet an officer at school with his head teacher and his mum, and say sorry to the shopkeeper face to face. A week later Maddie was in a park scuffle when a police car pulled up; everyone scattered except Maddie, who stood there with a muddy knee. Her dad said: «If the police come round again, you lose your phone for a month.»
explain: Not every visit was bad news. Sam saw a fight in the street, and an officer asked him what he had seen. His voice wobbled, but he said only what he really saw — not what other kids said, not guesses. And one evening Lily saw older boys fighting near her house; when one of them lay still on the pavement and didn't get up, she rang 999 and stayed on the line from her doorway until the ambulance came. The officer said she had done exactly the right thing.
explain: Police are not only there to catch people; they keep people safe and stop things getting worse. Fights in the street are a police matter because people get badly hurt and property gets broken. Being honest with the police, staying calm, and calling 999 when someone is hurt or in danger are what sensible people do. Later that term a community police officer came to Jake's school to talk about staying safe and street fights.
fact: In the UK the emergency number is 999 (112 also works); in the USA and Canada it is 911, in Australia 000 and in New Zealand 111.
fact: 999 reaches the police, an ambulance, the fire service or the coastguard (who rescue people at sea).
fact: You call 999 when someone is badly hurt, in danger, or a crime is happening right now.
fact: A person lying still after a fight may be seriously hurt and needs an ambulance quickly.
fact: When you call 999, you say which service you need, where you are, and what has happened, and you answer the operator's questions.
fact: Stay at a safe distance from a fight; your job is to call for help, not to step in.
fact: A witness should say only what they really saw and heard, not guesses or what others said.
fact: Standing by laughing while someone commits a crime can still get you questioned by the police.
fact: In England and Wales, children aged 10 and over can be charged with a crime.
fact: Damaging someone's property during a fight, like a shop stand, usually has to be paid for or put right.
fact: Community police officers visit schools to help children stay safe, not only to catch people.
fact: Telling the police the truth calmly is always the best choice; lying makes things much worse.
fact: Parents often set their own consequences, like losing a phone, on top of anything the police do.
exercise: Why did the officer come to Kieran's door? | He had been there when a bike was stolen | He had won a prize for good behaviour at school | His mum had lost her keys | She was looking for a lost dog
exercise: Kieran didn't steal the bike himself. Why was he still asked questions? | He was there laughing while it happened | Police always ask every single child on the street | He looked like the thief | His bike was the same colour
exercise: What did the officer mean by «just a name, not a record»? | So far it is only a note, and he should keep out of trouble | He would be in prison by next week | He would get a medal for honesty | He would never be allowed in the Co-op again, not even with his mum
exercise: How did Kieran's mum react? | She stayed silent all evening, which felt worse than shouting | She laughed and said it didn't matter one bit | She bought him a brand new bike to cheer him up and keep him at home | She told the officer to go away
exercise: Why did the shopkeeper call the police on Jake? | Jake's scrap knocked over his stand and spilled his fruit | Jake had asked the shopkeeper for some free oranges for his tea | Jake was standing too close to the door | The shopkeeper wanted to give Jake a Saturday job
exercise: What did Jake have to do afterwards? | Meet an officer with his head and mum, and say sorry to the shopkeeper | Nothing at all, because oranges are cheap and the stand was outside anyway | Pay a fine of a thousand pounds | Go to prison for a week
exercise: When the police car stopped at the park, what did Maddie do? | She stood still instead of running off | She ran off faster than everyone else did | She hid in the bushes by the wall | She climbed a tree
exercise: What did Maddie's dad say would happen if the police came again? | She would lose her phone for a month | She would get a new phone for being honest | She would be sent to live with Gran for a while | Nothing at all, because she is a girl
exercise: What did Sam do when the officer asked what he saw? | Said only what he had really seen | Repeated what the other kids said | Guessed who had started it, to be helpful | Said he saw nothing to stay out of it
exercise: Why should a witness not guess? | A wrong guess could blame the wrong person | Guessing makes the officer's job a lot easier and quicker | Guesses are always right | The police prefer exciting stories
exercise: Why did Lily call 999? | A boy was lying still after the fight | The boys were far too noisy outside her house | She wanted the police to see her house | The fight was on her favourite TV show
exercise: Where did Lily stay while she was on the phone? | At a safe distance, in her doorway | In the middle of the fight | Right next to the boys, to stop them | Inside with the door locked and the phone off
exercise: What does a 999 caller need to say? | Which service, where they are and what happened | Only their own name and how old they are, nothing else | Only that it is urgent, then hang up quickly | Their school and their year group
exercise: Why did a community police officer come to the school? | To talk about staying safe and street fights | To arrest the whole class at once | To check everyone's homework for mistakes | To give out speeding tickets
exercise: In England and Wales, from what age can a child be charged with a crime? | From age 10 | From age eighteen | From age sixteen | From age five
exercise: What is the main job of the police in a street fight? | To keep people safe and stop it getting worse | To pick the winner of the fight and give out a prize | To film it for the news | To join in on the right side
test: older kid: «if the feds come, just leg it». Wise? | No — stay calm and tell the truth | Yeah, they can't catch you if you're quick | Only if you're fast | Leg it then hide at home
test: Mate: «tell the copper u never saw nothing, don't be a grass». What's right for a witness? | Say honestly what you actually saw | Say you saw nothing at all, to be safe | Make up a different, more exciting story | Blame someone you don't like very much
test: A lad's on the floor after a fight by the chippy and isn't moving. Everyone's filming. What do you do? | Ring 999 and stay back at a safe distance | Film it too, so the police can see it later | Shake him hard to wake him up as quickly as you can | Go home and say nothing
test: On a 999 call: «Which service?» What do you say? | Ambulance, and police — a boy's hurt after a fight on Elm Road | I dunno, someone's hurt somewhere near the shops, I think | Can you just come quick | Is this the police? I'll ring back
test: «I only watched, I didn't nick the bike, so nobody can say nothing to me». True? | Not quite — police can still question people who were there | Yes, people who only stood and watched are never asked anything | Yes, if you were laughing | Only if you're under 18
test: Teacher: «Jake, what do you say to Mr Patel about his oranges?» Best answer? | Sorry — I'll help clear up and pay my share | It wasn't me, it was the other lad who knocked it | Oranges are cheap anyway | He shouldn't put stands outside
test: Dad: «police again and the phone's gone for a month». What's he trying to do? | Make sure Maddie stays out of fights | Punish her for nothing at all | Stop her going out with friends | Get himself a new phone
test: Kieran: «my name's in the copper's book, I'm a proper criminal now lol». What's true? | It's a warning sign, not something to be proud of | He's famous now, everyone knows his name | He'll get a cool gang name for it | It means he can't be told off any more
test: Sam's hands are shaking when the officer asks him questions. What helps most? | Breathe, go slowly, and say only what he really saw | Say whatever he thinks the officer wants to hear from him | Say he forgot everything | Say his friend saw it instead of him
test: «999 is only for fires innit». Correct? | No, it's for police, ambulance, fire and coastguard | Yes, it's only for fires and nothing else | Only for robberies | Only if an adult rings
test: Community officer: «What should you do if you see a fight?» Best answer? | Keep away, get an adult or call 999 if someone's hurt | Jump straight in between them and split them up yourself | Cheer for the smaller one | Throw something at them to stop it
test: «police cars only stop when someone's getting arrested». True? | No, they also stop to check people are safe | Yes, always, that's the only thing they're there for | Only at night | Only for adults
exam: It is given that a person lies motionless following an altercation in a public street. Identify the action most consistent with established guidance. | The summoning of emergency services by telephone while remaining at a safe distance | The physical intervention of the observer between the parties concerned | The recording of the incident upon a mobile device for later submission to the police | The departure of the observer without informing any person
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the obligation of a child witness questioned by a police officer? | The witness should state truthfully only what he personally saw and heard | The witness should relate the accounts of others as his own | The witness should decline to answer in order to protect his peers from blame | The witness should supply probable details where uncertain
exam: State the minimum age at which a child may be charged with a criminal offence in England and Wales. | Ten years | Eighteen years | Sixteen years | Five years
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a child who was present and amused during the theft of a bicycle. | Presence and encouragement may result in the child being questioned by the police | Presence without participation is beyond the interest of the police | Only the adult guardian may be questioned | The child is thereby rendered criminally liable irrespective of age
